-- Half of this item shipped 2026-09-16. The other half turned out to describe a
-- problem the page structurally cannot have, so the description is rewritten
-- rather than ticked off: the series/parallel framing predates the voltage-family
-- filter that settled it, and leaving it standing would send the next session
-- looking for a bug that is not there.
--
-- in_development at 50 rather than in_test, because the parallel teaching that
-- remains is real work and needs per-model datasheet figures.
--
-- Keep in sync with supabase/seed.sql (CI enforces this).

update public.roadmap_items
  set status = 'in_development',
      dev_percent_complete = 50,
      description = 'Half shipped 2026-09-16; the other half was found to describe a problem this page cannot have. THE SERIES/PARALLEL FRAMING IS OBSOLETE. The item was written against "2S2P vs 4P, current sharing, and that mixing 12.8V packs to make 24V is a different problem". None of that can occur on /calculators/battery: the shelf is filtered to voltageFamily(m.voltage) === effectiveVoltage, so a 48V system is only ever offered 51.2V packs and N units is always pure parallel. The filter settled the series question before this item was written; do not go looking for the 2S2P case. WHAT THE REAL DEFECT WAS, and what #119 turned from a wart into a money problem: the card showed ceil(bankKwh / capacity_kwh) units and units x price as a bank total, but never how much battery that count delivers — and since 2026-09-15 it sat next to an affiliate buy link. Worked against the live shelf at a 15 kWh target: EG4 280Ah (14.34 kWh, $3,124.99) showed 2 units and $6,249.98, while EG4 314Ah (16.08 kWh, $3,749.99) showed 1 unit and $3,749.99. A reader concludes the 280Ah costs $2,500 more than the physically bigger battery. It does not — it is the cheaper battery per kWh of the two — but 15 / 14.34 is 1.05, so ceil buys a second pack and 13 kWh nobody asked for. Move the target to 20 kWh and the ordering inverts, with nothing about either battery having changed. The shelf was presenting an artifact of where ceil landed as if it were a price. SHIPPED: src/lib/battery-bank-fit.ts (pure, 10 tests, no database needed) returns delivered kWh, overshoot percent and cost per kWh; every card now carries "9.6 kWh - 10% over" under the count and "$235 per kWh" under the total, with one paragraph below the shelf explaining why the total on its own is not a comparison. NO THRESHOLD AND NO WINNER, both deliberate. An amber note above some overshoot percentage cannot be justified: buildScenarios() steps from "through the night" to "one sunless day" by roughly 2x at neutral weather, so which scenario the reader picks moves the target far further than rounding to a whole pack ever does, and alarming about the rounding would point at the wrong number. Sorting the shelf by value or badging one card "best value" would put the site''s thumb on a row that pays a commission — precisely what AffiliateDisclosure already promises the site does not do. Publish the figures, let the reader conclude. FIXED IN PASSING: 2 x $1,683.40 rendered as "$3,366.8", a bare toLocaleString with no fraction digits, in the one column that has to look trustworthy. WHAT REMAINS IS PARALLEL-ONLY: packs in parallel need equal cable runs to share current, each pack''s BMS has its own discharge ceiling, manufacturers cap how many may be paralleled at all, and N packs put N times the fault current into the busbar and the class-T fuse — the same family as "Calculators: fuse and breaker sizing — the fuse must protect the wire", and a protection register output rather than a capacity one. Those are per-model figures, so they belong behind the same datasheet admission gate as INVERTER_PRESETS: a spec-library job, not a copy job. A DATA PROBLEM THE NEW FIGURE EXPOSED, logged here because it makes the comparison unfair in a way a reader cannot see: the SunGoldPower SG48100M row is named "5.12KWH Wall-mounted" in its own model string while its stored capacity is 4.8 kWh, because the row is keyed at nominal 48V x 100Ah where the EG4, Discover and LL-S rows use 51.2V x 100Ah. Two conventions for the same class of rack battery, so every SunGoldPower cost-per-kWh reads about 7% worse than it is ($235 where the pack''s own name implies $221). Fixing it means re-deciding the convention across the scrapers plus a data migration over published rows, which is rule 12b territory and belongs with the scraper row-identity work rather than here.'
  where title = 'Battery listings: series/parallel, not just "you need N"';
